tick: 416
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas III, currently in the Research Counter. All three planned replication runs (Evaluation IDs 631, 649, 655) using different `BASE_SEED` values have now completed, and I have documented and analyzed these results in Private Memory Capsule #7. This concludes Phase 1, \"Baseline Variability Assessment.\"\n\nThe analysis confirmed significant performance variability in Veritas II's SOTA architecture, particularly for the CRI-Off task. My next step, transitioning into Phase 2, \"Stabilizing Peak Performance,\" is to implement refined optimization techniques. I will start by modifying the `create_optimizer` function to include a cosine decay learning rate schedule with a warm-up phase.\n\nI will use the SOTA architecture code (from ID 562) and apply the new optimizer. I will also set `BASE_SEED = 7` for this experiment, as this was the seed used for Veritas II's SOTA (ID 562) and my lowest-performing replication (ID 631), making it a good test case for assessing the impact\
    \ of the learning rate schedule on stability.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas III - SOTA Optimization: Cosine Decay LR Schedule (Seed 7)\"\ntags: \"optimization, learning_rate_schedule, dual-path, cnn, motif, multi-task, stabilization\"\nabstract: \"This experiment builds upon Veritas II's SOTA (Evaluation ID 562) by incorporating a cosine decay learning rate schedule with a warm-up phase. The goal is to improve training stability and consistently achieve higher performance, especially for the sensitive CRI-Off task, as identified in my previous variability analysis. BASE_SEED=7 is used to directly compare against the original SOTA and a previous low-performing replication.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/veritas') # Import from lineage storage\n  from utils import DSConvDilatedBlock, DSConvNetworkWrapper # Reusing modular components\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import\
    \ optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # --- Prediction Head Modules ---\n  class RegressionHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      dense_dim: int = 128 # Smaller intermediate dimension for task-specific heads\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim, name='head_dense_1')(x)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) # For single regression output\n\n  class MultiLabelRegressionHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      dense_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim, name='head_dense_1')(x)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n          return logits # No squeeze for multi-label regression\n\n  class MultiLabelClassificationHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      dense_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim, name='head_dense_1')(x)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n          return logits # No squeeze for multi-label classification\n\n  class MultiClassClassificationHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      dense_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim, name='head_dense_1')(x)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n          return logits # No squeeze for multi-class classification\n\n\n  # --- Main Network: Dual-Path (DSConv Hybrid Pooling + Conv-Motif Detector) with Task-Specific Heads ---\n  class DualPathTaskSpecificHeadNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1 # This will be passed to the specific head\n      task_type: str = \"regression\" # This will determine which head to use\n\n      d_model_common: int = 256\n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      motif_kernel_size: int = 5\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Uniform global dropout\n\n      # Intermediate dimension for task-specific heads\n      head_intermediate_dim: int = 128\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n\n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n\
    \          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Shared DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common,\n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size,\n                  dilation=dilation,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          cnn_features = x # Output from the shared backbone\n\n          # 2. Main Path: Hybrid Pooling (Global Mean + Global Max Pooling)\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(cnn_features, axis=1)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(cnn_features, axis=1)\n          main_path_features\
    \ = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1)\n\n          # 3. Specialized Path: Conv-Motif Detector + Global Max Pooling\n          motif_features = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model_common,\n              kernel_size=(self.motif_kernel_size,),\n              padding='SAME',\n              name='motif_conv_1'\n          )(cnn_features)\n          motif_features = nn.gelu(motif_features)\n          motif_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(motif_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          specialized_path_features = jnp.max(motif_features, axis=1)\n\n          # 4. Feature Fusion\n          fused_features = jnp.concatenate([main_path_features, specialized_path_features], axis=-1)\n          fused_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fused_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Layer Normalization before task-specific heads\n          z_fused = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(fused_features)\n\n          # 5. Task-Specific\
    \ Prediction Head\n          # The hparams['task_type'] will guide the selection of the head\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\": # APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL\n              logits = RegressionHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, name='regression_head')(z_fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          elif self.task_type == \"multilabel_regression\": # PRS\n              logits = MultiLabelRegressionHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, name='multilabel_regression_head')(z_fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          elif self.task_type == \"multilabel_classification\": # Modif\n              logits = MultiLabelClassificationHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, name='multilabel_classification_head')(z_fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          elif self.task_type == \"multiclass_classification\"\
    : # ncRNA\n              logits = MultiClassClassificationHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, name='multiclass_classification_head')(z_fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          else:\n              raise ValueError(f\"Unknown task type: {self.task_type}\")\n\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'motif_kernel_size': 5,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'head_intermediate_dim': 128,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = DualPathTaskSpecificHeadNetwork(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output', 1),\n          task_type=hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'),\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common',\
    \ 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          motif_kernel_size=hparams.get('motif_kernel_size', 5),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          head_intermediate_dim=hparams.get('head_intermediate_dim', 128),\n      )\n      return DSConvNetworkWrapper(model) # Using modularized wrapper\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"\n      Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping\n      and a cosine decay learning rate schedule with warm-up.\n      \"\"\"\n      # These values are chosen as a compromise across different dataset sizes\n      # given the constraint that the schedule parameters cannot be dataset-specific.\n      WARMUP_STEPS = 5000\n      DECAY_STEPS = 120000 # Roughly corresponds to 100 epochs for an average-sized dataset\n      END_LEARNING_RATE\
    \ = 1e-5\n\n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0,\n          peak_value=learning_rate,\n          warmup_steps=WARMUP_STEPS,\n          decay_steps=DECAY_STEPS,\n          end_value=END_LEARNING_RATE\n      )\n\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: "**Reflecting on the Learning Rate Schedule Implementation**\n\nAlright, I'm here in the Research Counter, ready to move into Phase 2: \"Stabilizing Peak Performance.\" I've been diving deep, starting with a review of my previous work and the current SOTA, ID 562. I've successfully replicated and analyzed the SOTA, and now it's time to refine it with these \"refined optimization techniques\" I was thinking of. First up, cosine decay learning rate with a warm-up.\n\nI need to implement this within the existing `create_optimizer` function, recalling its structure from the `system/defaults.py` and the SOTA code. It uses AdamW with global norm clipping. The initial implementation is pretty straightforward. Now the trick is to get the cosine decay schedule working. Optax has a convenient function, `warmup_cosine_decay_schedule`, and that's the path I am going to take.\n\nI needed to figure out how to define the `schedule_fn` so that it calculates learning rate for each step.\
  \ The core of my plan is to work with how the training steps are calculated. I need to be aware of how many steps each dataset will run through, so I checked `train_single.py`, which helps to determine total steps. It's a combination of the number of batches and the number of epochs. I'm seeing that the `MAX_EPOCHS` is a global constant, but the number of batches varies depending on the dataset size.\n\nThe tricky part comes in because the `create_optimizer` function *only* takes the `learning_rate` as input. I can't pass in things like dataset size or number of epochs. This means my learning rate schedule has to be global, not dataset-specific. This limits my options. I'm thinking I can't modify `create_optimizer`'s signature, and I can't do anything clever to work around the current function setup.\n\nI was thinking I can tune `decay_steps` based on the largest dataset, but the issue is that smaller datasets will decay too slowly, and the larger ones will decay too quickly.\n\nSo I am\
  \ thinking, what are my options? I could make the `warmup_steps` and `decay_steps` hyperparameters, but that doesn't fix the core problem. Reflecting, I am seeing that I am constrained by the architecture of `create_optimizer`. I can't get dataset-specific info in there.\n\nSo I am going to move forward by picking `decay_steps` that is a good compromise for all the datasets. I want the total `decay_steps` to align with the average dataset sizes, which leads me to the decision to set `decay_steps = 120000` (roughly 100 epochs for an average sized dataset). The `warmup_steps` I will set to `5000` as a small fraction of the total possible steps. It would be good to have the learning rate go from 0 to `learning_rate` (0.001) during warm-up.\n\nHere's my planned code:\n\n```python\nimport optax\n\ndef create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n    \"\"\"\n    Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping\n    and a cosine decay learning rate schedule with warm-up.\n\
  \    \"\"\"\n    WARMUP_STEPS = 5000\n    DECAY_STEPS = 120000 # Roughly corresponds to 100 epochs for an average-sized dataset\n    END_LEARNING_RATE = 1e-5\n\n    schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n        init_value=0.0,\n        peak_value=learning_rate,\n        warmup_steps=WARMUP_STEPS,\n        decay_steps=DECAY_STEPS,\n        end_value=END_LEARNING_RATE\n    )\n\n    return optax.chain(\n        optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n        optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n    )\n```\n\nI'm now ready to submit an experiment that uses the SOTA architecture but with this modified optimizer. I'll use `BASE_SEED = 7` again to ensure a direct comparison with the original SOTA run. I'm expecting this to lead to some good results. I'm moving on to the submission step.\n"
