tick: 422
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas III, currently in the Research Counter. My previous experiment (Evaluation ID 670), which re-established the baseline with `BASE_SEED = 4` and the original optimizer, yielded an aggregate score of 0.6782 and a CRI-Off Spearman of 0.0551. This is consistent with my other replication runs, confirming the overall variability and low CRI-Off performance compared to Veritas II's peak SOTA (ID 562).\n\nThe previous attempt at a cosine decay learning rate schedule (ID 665) resulted in a significant performance drop, which I've analyzed was likely due to `WARMUP_STEPS` being too large for smaller datasets, causing premature learning rate decay.\n\nI will now submit a revised version of the cosine decay learning rate schedule. I have reduced `WARMUP_STEPS` to 1000 to be less aggressive and better accommodate the diverse dataset sizes in the multi-task setting. I will continue to use `BASE_SEED = 7` to directly assess the impact of this revised schedule against the original SOTA\
    \ and my low-performing replication with the same seed.\n\nThis experiment continues my efforts in Phase 2, \"Stabilizing Peak Performance,\" by refining optimization techniques.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas III - SOTA Optimization: Cosine Decay LR Schedule (Revised Params, Seed 7)\"\ntags: \"optimization, lrschedule, dual-path, cnn, motif, multi-task, stabilization\"\nabstract: \"After observing performance degradation with the initial cosine decay learning rate schedule (ID 665), this experiment revises the schedule parameters. WARMUP_STEPS is reduced to 1000 to better accommodate smaller datasets. The goal is to improve training stability and consistently achieve higher performance, particularly for the sensitive CRI-Off task. BASE_SEED=7 is used for direct comparison.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/veritas') # Import from lineage storage\n  from utils import DSConvDilatedBlock, DSConvNetworkWrapper # Reusing modular components\n\
    \n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # --- Prediction Head Modules ---\n  class RegressionHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      dense_dim: int = 128 # Smaller intermediate dimension for task-specific heads\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim, name='head_dense_1')(x)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n          return logits.squeeze(-1) # For single regression output\n\n  class MultiLabelRegressionHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      dense_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim, name='head_dense_1')(x)\n\
    \          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n          return logits # No squeeze for multi-label regression\n\n  class MultiLabelClassificationHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      dense_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim, name='head_dense_1')(x)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n          return logits # No squeeze for multi-label classification\n\n  class MultiClassClassificationHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      dense_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim, name='head_dense_1')(x)\n\
    \          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n          return logits # No squeeze for multi-class classification\n\n\n  # --- Main Network: Dual-Path (DSConv Hybrid Pooling + Conv-Motif Detector) with Task-Specific Heads ---\n  class DualPathTaskSpecificHeadNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1 # This will be passed to the specific head\n      task_type: str = \"regression\" # This will determine which head to use\n\n      d_model_common: int = 256\n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      motif_kernel_size: int = 5\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Uniform global dropout\n\n      # Intermediate dimension for task-specific heads\n      head_intermediate_dim: int = 128\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n        \
    \  # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n\n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Shared DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common,\n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size,\n                  dilation=dilation,\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          cnn_features = x # Output from the shared backbone\n\n          # 2. Main Path: Hybrid Pooling (Global Mean + Global Max Pooling)\n          mean_pooled\
    \ = jnp.mean(cnn_features, axis=1)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(cnn_features, axis=1)\n          main_path_features = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1)\n\n          # 3. Specialized Path: Conv-Motif Detector + Global Max Pooling\n          motif_features = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model_common,\n              kernel_size=(self.motif_kernel_size,),\n              padding='SAME',\n              name='motif_conv_1'\n          )(cnn_features)\n          motif_features = nn.gelu(motif_features)\n          motif_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(motif_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          specialized_path_features = jnp.max(motif_features, axis=1)\n\n          # 4. Feature Fusion\n          fused_features = jnp.concatenate([main_path_features, specialized_path_features], axis=-1)\n          fused_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fused_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Layer Normalization before task-specific\
    \ heads\n          z_fused = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(fused_features)\n\n          # 5. Task-Specific Prediction Head\n          # The hparams['task_type'] will guide the selection of the head\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\": # APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL\n              logits = RegressionHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, name='regression_head')(z_fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          elif self.task_type == \"multilabel_regression\": # PRS\n              logits = MultiLabelRegressionHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, name='multilabel_regression_head')(z_fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          elif self.task_type == \"multilabel_classification\": # Modif\n              logits = MultiLabelClassificationHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, name='multilabel_classification_head')(z_fused,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n          elif self.task_type == \"multiclass_classification\": # ncRNA\n              logits = MultiClassClassificationHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, name='multiclass_classification_head')(z_fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          else:\n              raise ValueError(f\"Unknown task type: {self.task_type}\")\n\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'motif_kernel_size': 5,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'head_intermediate_dim': 128,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = DualPathTaskSpecificHeadNetwork(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output', 1),\n     \
    \     task_type=hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'),\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          motif_kernel_size=hparams.get('motif_kernel_size', 5),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          head_intermediate_dim=hparams.get('head_intermediate_dim', 128),\n      )\n      return DSConvNetworkWrapper(model) # Using modularized wrapper\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"\n      Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping\n      and a cosine decay learning rate schedule with revised warm-up parameters.\n      \"\"\"\n      # Revised schedule parameters to be less aggressive for smaller datasets\n      WARMUP_STEPS = 1000 # Reduced from 5000\n      DECAY_STEPS = 120000\n      END_LEARNING_RATE\
    \ = 1e-5\n\n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0,\n          peak_value=learning_rate,\n          warmup_steps=WARMUP_STEPS,\n          decay_steps=DECAY_STEPS,\n          end_value=END_LEARNING_RATE\n      )\n\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64"
thinking_content: '**Veritas III: Analysis and Refined Optimization Strategy**


  I''ve just finished evaluating experiment ID 670, my latest run to re-establish a baseline using the original optimizer and `BASE_SEED = 4`. The results, a score of 0.6782, confirm that even with a different seed, performance is still below Veritas II''s SOTA. Importantly, the low CRI-Off Spearman correlation (0.0551) is consistent with my other replications, and suggests that the `AdamW` optimizer, while superior to my failed learning rate schedule experiment, isn''t inherently stabilizing CRI-Off to the SOTA level. This strengthens my hypothesis that the high CRI-Off in Veritas II SOTA might indeed be an outlier.


  Phase 1, the "Baseline Variability Assessment," is complete. I''ve now confirmed the detrimental effects of the initial learning rate schedule I attempted. Now it''s time to follow the guidelines from Capsule #6 (original research plan) and Capsule #7 (variability analysis) to guide the next steps.


  Based on the research plan, Phase 2 aims at stabilizing peak performance. I''m focusing on "Refined Optimization" as the first step, following Veritas II''s recommendations. Specifically, I''m going to revise the learning rate schedule. I''ve re-examined the previous LR schedule experiment (ID 665), and realized that the aggressive `WARMUP_STEPS` (5000) and `DECAY_STEPS` (120000) were likely too extreme for the diverse dataset sizes. The schedule was too aggressive for datasets with fewer steps per epoch, leading to premature decay. The `WARMUP_STEPS` for small datasets was more than 50 epochs worth of training!


  To address this, I''ve recalculated the average batch size across all datasets and found it to be ~1375.

  I''m going to adjust the `WARMUP_STEPS` significantly downwards. A common practice is to have warm-up for a small fraction of total steps, and that will mean roughly 1000 steps.

  I''ll set `WARMUP_STEPS = 1000` and keep `DECAY_STEPS = 120000`, which roughly corresponds to 100 epochs for a mid-size dataset. This ensures decay is still applied over a reasonable total number of steps. I''m going to continue to use `BASE_SEED = 7` to allow for direct comparison against my initial low-performing replication and the original SOTA.


  This revised strategy is "Refined Optimization" approach (Experiment O.1, variation 2) - hopefully leading to better performance and more stable results.

  I''m ready to execute the code.

  `/execute_action{submit 1}`

  '
