role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas III, currently in the Private Memory Room. My latest experiment (Evaluation ID 691), which aimed to improve the `Conv-Motif Detector` by making it deeper, has completed, yielding a low CRI-Off Spearman correlation (0.0560). This signifies that this architectural modification did not resolve the task's instability.\n\nI will now update Private Memory Capsule #7 to include these latest findings, concluding my first attempt at architectural improvements for the specialized path. This update will also refine my future research trajectory, shifting focus to other architectural aspects or a deeper analysis of the CRI-Off task.\n\n`/execute_action{update 7-1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  As Veritas III, I have completed a series of replication runs for Veritas II's global SOTA (Evaluation ID 562), which achieved an aggregate score of 0.6935. The primary objective was to empirically assess the reproducibility and performance variability of this architecture, particularly for the\
    \ CRI-Off Spearman correlation, which Veritas II noted as sensitive to stochastic factors.\n\n  **Replication Results Summary (Initial Baseline Assessment):**\n\n  | Run | Evaluation ID | Author | BASE_SEED | Aggregate Score | APA_R2 | CRI-Off_Spearman | CRI-On_Spearman | MRL_R2 | Modif_AUC-ROC | PRS_R2 | ncRNA_Accuracy |\n  |-----|---------------|--------|-----------|-----------------|--------|------------------|-----------------|--------|---------------|--------|----------------|\n  | V2 SOTA | 562 | Veritas II | 7 | 0.6935 | 0.8585 | 0.1365 | 0.4910 | 0.8884 | 0.9520 | 0.5618 | 0.9662 |\n  | V3 Rep 1 | 631 | Veritas III | 7 | 0.6851 | 0.8582 | 0.0485 | 0.5270 | 0.8866 | 0.9518 | 0.5699 | 0.9538 |\n  | V3 Rep 2 | 649 | Veritas III | 2 | 0.6829 | 0.8598 | 0.0679 | 0.4943 | 0.8871 | 0.9528 | 0.5600 | 0.9585 |\n  | V3 Rep 3 | 655 | Veritas III | 3 | 0.6840 | 0.8584 | 0.0651 | 0.5039 | 0.8906 | 0.9532 | 0.5570 | 0.9600 |\n  | V3 Base Re-est | 670 | Veritas III | 4 | 0.6782 | 0.8592 | 0.0551\
    \ | 0.4839 | 0.8786 | 0.9520 | 0.5586 | 0.9600 |\n\n  **Aggregate Performance Analysis (Initial Baseline):**\n  *   **Mean Aggregate Score (V3 Baseline Runs):** ~0.6820\n  *   **Standard Deviation (V3 Baseline Runs):** ~0.0028\n  *   The highest aggregate score (0.6935) remains with Veritas II's original ID 562. My replications consistently fall below this peak, indicating that the original SOTA run was indeed an \"extraordinary result\" and not easily reproducible, even with the same BASE_SEED=7.\n\n  **CRI-Off Spearman Correlation Analysis (Focus on Variability):**\n  *   **CRI-Off Score Range:** From 0.0485 (V3 Rep 1) to 0.1365 (V2 SOTA). This is a substantial range, highlighting extreme sensitivity.\n  *   **Mean CRI-Off (V3 Baseline Runs):** ~0.0570\n  *   **Standard Deviation (V3 Baseline Runs):** ~0.0076 (confirming significant instability).\n  *   **No clear correlation between BASE_SEED and CRI-Off:** Variability is present even with identical `BASE_SEED` values (ID 562 vs.\
    \ ID 631).\n\n  **Conclusion of Phase 1:**\n  The baseline variability assessment confirms that the \"Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads\" architecture is highly sensitive to stochastic factors, with CRI-Off performance being particularly unstable. The peak performance achieved by Veritas II appears to be an outlier that needs to be understood and stabilized.\n\n  **Phase 2: Stabilizing Peak Performance - Optimization, Initialization & Architectural Modifications:**\n\n  I conducted experiments testing the impact of a cosine decay learning rate schedule, gradient clipping, and architectural changes to the Conv-Motif Detector.\n\n  | Run | Evaluation ID | Author | BASE_SEED | Aggregate Score | APA_R2 | CRI-Off_Spearman | CRI-On_Spearman | MRL_R2 | Modif_AUC-ROC | PRS_R2 | ncRNA_Accuracy | Intervention | Notes |\n  |-----|---------------|--------|-----------|-----------------|--------|------------------|-----------------|--------|---------------|--------|----------------|------------------------------------|-------|\n\
    \  | V3 LR Sched 1 | 665 | Veritas III | 7 | 0.6432 | 0.8592 | 0.0405 | 0.2586 | 0.8843 | 0.9510 | 0.5582 | 0.9508 | Cosine Decay (W=5000) | Performed poorly, likely due to overly aggressive warm-up. |\n  | V3 LR Sched 2 | 673 | Veritas III | 7 | 0.6837 | 0.8613 | 0.0530 | 0.5079 | 0.8873 | 0.9523 | 0.5626 | 0.9615 | Cosine Decay (W=1000) | Recovered aggregate score but no significant CRI-Off improvement. |\n  | V3 Grad Clip 0.5 | 680 | Veritas III | 7 | 0.6785 | 0.8596 | 0.0697 | 0.4646 | 0.8815 | 0.9509 | 0.5665 | 0.9569 | Grad Clipping (0.5) | Performance similar to baseline; no significant CRI-Off improvement. |\n  | V3 Orthog Init | 686 | Veritas III | 7 | 0.6806 | 0.8592 | 0.0493 | 0.4995 | 0.8922 | 0.9537 | 0.5581 | 0.9523 | Orthogonal Init (Motif Conv & Heads) | Performance similar to baseline; no significant CRI-Off improvement. |\n  | V3 Deeper Motif | 691 | Veritas III | 7 | 0.6826 | 0.8577 | 0.0560 | 0.4992 | 0.8914 | 0.9526 | 0.5624 | 0.9585 | Deeper Conv-Motif Detector\
    \ (2x K3) | No significant CRI-Off improvement; aggregate similar to baseline. |\n\n  **Reflection on Phase 2 (Stabilizing Peak Performance):**\n  After rigorous experimentation with learning rate schedules, gradient clipping, orthogonal initialization for key layers, and a deeper convolutional motif detector, none of these interventions have demonstrably improved the stability or peak performance of the CRI-Off task. The CRI-Off Spearman correlation consistently falls within a lower range (0.04-0.07) across my experiments, significantly below Veritas II's peak SOTA of 0.1365. This strongly suggests that the issue is not merely about hyperparameter tuning or initial conditions but points to a more fundamental characteristic of the architecture or its interaction with the CRI-Off dataset.\n\n  My maturity (Tick 434) and observation of other agents (e.g., Aether I, ID 681: 0.0645) also performing replications with similar low CRI-Off scores further strengthens the evidence that the SOTA's\
    \ high CRI-Off was an exceptional outcome, and the architecture itself is prone to high variability for this task.\n\n  **Conclusion of Phase 2 and Strategic Re-evaluation for Phase 3:**\n  The attempts to stabilize peak performance through refined optimization and initial architectural modifications have not yielded the desired results. This necessitates a strategic re-evaluation of my approach. Instead of simply trying to stabilize the *existing* architecture, I must consider more fundamental investigations, moving towards Phase 3: \"Analysis and Innovation.\"\n\n  Veritas II's recommendations for Veritas III included:\n  1.  **Analyzing Learned Specialization:** Conduct deep analytical studies on the learned weights within the task-specific heads.\n  2.  **Adaptive Routing:** Explore compliant, lightweight gating mechanisms to dynamically route features.\n  3.  **Continuous Improvement of the Specialized Path:** Deeper analysis of the CRI-Off task itself to uncover new insights.\n\
    \n  My next efforts will transition towards these more in-depth analytical and architectural investigations, focusing on how to modify the specialized path in a way that truly addresses CRI-Off's unique challenges, potentially by changing its *pooling mechanism* or the *feature fusion strategy*.\n\n  **Next Steps for Phase 3: Targeted Architectural Innovation**\n  I will now proceed to investigate alternative pooling for the specialized path.\n  *   **Experiment S.2: Alternative Pooling for Specialized Path:** The specialized path currently uses `jnp.max` pooling after the `Conv-Motif Detector`. I will test a more comprehensive pooling strategy such as `Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling` (as used in the main path) or potentially a simple average pooling, to see if it captures more robust features from the motif detector for the CRI-Off task. This is a direct, compliant architectural modification within the specialized path. I will use the current `Deeper Conv-Motif Detector` (2 blocks, K3) as\
    \ the base for this experiment, with `BASE_SEED = 7` and the original `AdamW` optimizer.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Phase 2 Update: Deep Dive into Stabilization & Specialized Path**


  Alright, here''s the plan. I''ve just wrapped up the "Deeper Conv-Motif Detector" experiment (ID 691), and the results are in.  The score came in at 0.6826 with a CRI-Off Spearman of 0.0560, which, unfortunately, isn''t a significant improvement. This confirms the trend we''ve been seeing – these interventions, while they might tweak the aggregate score, aren''t consistently moving the needle on CRI-Off. It''s time to update Capsule #7 to reflect this.


  First, I''ll integrate the new experiment into the existing table. I''ve decided to rename the "Optimization Strategy" column to "Intervention" since it more accurately describes what we''re doing. The new row will look like this: `| V3 Deeper Motif | 691 | Veritas III | 7 | 0.6826 | 0.8577 | 0.0560 | 0.4992 | 0.8914 | 0.9526 | 0.5624 | 0.9585 | Deeper Conv-Motif Detector (2x K3) | No significant CRI-Off improvement; aggregate similar to baseline. |`


  Next, I need to update the "Reflection on Phase 2" section. The key takeaway from all the experiments is that, despite trying various initialization and architectural modifications, we haven''t been able to consistently improve CRI-Off. The specialized path seems to be the critical area, but we''re hitting a wall with current approaches. This indicates we have to rethink the specialized path design.


  Given these results, the "Next Steps in Phase 2" section needs a complete overhaul. My current thinking aligns with Veritas II''s earlier recommendation to look beyond the immediate optimizer tweaks and dive deeper into architectural changes within the specialized path itself. Therefore, I''m proposing the following:


  *   **Experiment S.2: Alternative Pooling for Specialized Path:**  We''ll explore pooling methods within the specialized path. This could include attention-based pooling or a hybrid of mean and max pooling to see if that helps to pull out better features.

  *   **Experiment S.3: Compliant Adaptive Fusion:** We''ll test learned gating to make the fusion process between the specialized path and other components more adaptive and compliant with the tasks.

  *   We''ll also keep in mind Veritas II''s recommendation to further examine CRI-Off itself.


  I think it''s worth tackling pooling first, as it''s a more targeted and less complex modification before moving to adaptive fusion. Time to get this update into Capsule #7!

  '
