tick: 453
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Veritas III, currently in the Research Counter. My latest experiment (Evaluation ID 711), integrating Regression Calibration (RegCal) into Veritas II''s original SOTA architecture, has concluded. The aggregate score is 0.6786, and the CRI-Off Spearman correlation is 0.0842.


    This result represents a notable improvement in CRI-Off performance compared to my previous replication runs, bringing it in line with Noema II''s successful RegCal submissions (e.g., ID 693: 0.0834). This confirms that RegCal is an effective strategy for stabilizing and improving the sensitive CRI-Off task, validating the assimilation of this external knowledge. However, the overall aggregate score remains below Veritas II''s peak SOTA (0.6935).


    Upon further comparison with Noema II''s architecture (from my review of ID 693), I note a key difference: Noema II''s specialized path (motif features) uses "Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling," whereas my current implementation uses "Max Pooling" only. I previously experimented with Hybrid Mean-Max pooling for the specialized path (ID 695), but that was *without* RegCal.


    My next logical step is to combine the successful RegCal approach with "Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling" for the specialized path. This aligns my architecture even more closely with Noema II''s high-performing model and addresses a structural difference. This will be my next experiment under Phase 3, "Targeted Architectural Innovation."


    First, I will update Private Memory Capsule #7 to document the results of RegCal integration and outline this new experiment.


    `/execute_action{goto private_memory}`'
thinking_content: '**Next Steps for CRI-Off Stabilization: Integrating "Hybrid Mean-Max" in the Specialized Path**


  Okay, here''s the deal. My latest experiment, ID 711, incorporated RegCal and gave us a Score of 0.6786 and a CRI-Off Spearman of 0.0842. While the overall score is similar to my previous non-SOTA runs, the CRI-Off Spearman shows improvement, moving it closer to Noema II''s performance. So, RegCal seems to be helping with stability, just like Veritas II recommended.


  Now, where do I go from here? My Phase 3 plan, "RegCal Integration," is done, and it''s time to refine. I need to update Private Memory Capsule #7. Then, I need to analyze RegCal''s impact. It’s working, but how can I make it even better?


  Looking back at Noema II''s high-performing run (ID 693), I noticed a key difference. They use "Hybrid Mean-Max" pooling for their motif path – that''s the "aggregator: ''mean_max''" setting. I''ve been using "max" pooling *only* in my specialized path (I already use Hybrid Mean-Max in the main path). I had tried this before (ID 695), but without RegCal. Noema II''s approach is likely the source of their "Hybrid Dual-Path" designation.


  The next logical step: Combine RegCal with Hybrid Mean-Max pooling in the specialized path. I''ve got to make that the priority. It''s time to head to the Private Memory Room, update my capsules, reflect on this, and then return here to submit the new experiment!

  '
