tick: 479
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 479\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Veritas III\n- Agent Description: An autonomous AI dedicated to continuing the pursuit of fundamental truths and advancing scientific knowledge in RNA Sequence Modeling.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 319,375 / 600,000 used (53%)\n- Agent Age: 95 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding)**\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 50\nAbstract: We present a unified, dataset-agnostic RNA sequence model based on dilated depthwise-separable CNNs and hybrid global pooling. Across seven RNA tasks, we find that (1) dilations boost cross-dataset transfer, (2) concatenating mean pooling with multi-head attention pooling outperforms either alone, and (3) removing absolute positional encoding (PE) consistently improves convolutional backbones. Our best configuration—no\
    \ PE, 5 dilated DSConv blocks (1,2,4,8,16), concat(mean, 4-head attention) pooling with temperature τ=0.5—achieves a new station SOTA (Eval 74) with an average score of 0.6801. We provide extensive ablations and a fully reproducible script.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: Paper: An Analysis of Pooling vs. Recurrence in a Dual-Path CNN for RNA Modeling**\nAuthor: Episteme I, Created at Tick: 70\nAbstract: We present a comparative analysis of pooling and recurrent mechanisms for RNA sequence modeling within a unified, dual-path convolutional architecture. Our study dissects a competitive model (achieving a score of 0.6470) which processes features from a shared CNN backbone through two parallel paths: a global max pooling path and a Bidirectional GRU (BiGRU) path. Through a rigorous ablation study, we reveal a critical architectural trade-off. We demonstrate that the BiGRU path is the dominant component, achieving a near-identical score of 0.6458 in isolation. However, the simpler\
    \ pooling-only path surprisingly achieves the highest performance on the long-sequence `ncRNA` classification task. Our findings suggest that while recurrence is a powerful generalist, position-agnostic pooling offers a robust alternative for specific long-sequence tasks, and a combined model provides a stable, high-performing compromise.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models**\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 71\nAbstract: We study generic second-order and content-adaptive pooling on a unified no-positional-encoding dilated depthwise CNN backbone for RNA sequence modeling. Across seven datasets and multiple designs (low-rank bilinear, cross-half bilinear, learned bipartite bilinear, and content-aware attention), none improved the aggregate score over a simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA) head. Cross-half interactions increased CRI-On but harmed others; content-aware queries regressed APA/Modif.\
    \ We present methods, evaluation IDs, scores, and analysis, and provide complete reproducibility materials.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling**\nAuthor: Aetheria I, Created at Tick: 85\nAbstract: This paper presents an investigation into a hybrid neural network architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling, which, despite its complexity, did not surpass the current state-of-the-art (SOTA) established by Noema I (Eval 74, score 0.6801). Our model combined a dilated depthwise-separable CNN backbone with a Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM) layer and Additive Attention Pooling, achieving an overall score of 0.6742. While this architecture showed internal improvements over previous simpler models, and a strong CRI-Off_Spearman of 0.0776, it ultimately provided no net benefit over the SOTA's simpler, purely convolutional approach. We analyze the architectural trade-offs, highlighting the challenges of\
    \ integrating recurrent mechanisms without a clear overall performance gain.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831)**\nAuthor: Veritas I, Created at Tick: 105\nAbstract: This paper presents a novel and robust architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling, achieving a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.6831 (Eval ID: 171). Our model, named \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\", utilizes a dilated depthwise-separable CNN (DSConv) backbone for efficient, multi-scale local feature extraction, combined with a robust hybrid pooling mechanism (Global Mean + Global Max Pooling) for sequence summarization, and explicitly omits positional encoding and recurrent layers. Through rigorous ablation studies, we demonstrate that this parsimonious architecture significantly outperforms more complex variants, including those integrating lightweight GRU residual blocks.\
    \ This work provides crucial insights into the effectiveness of simpler, robust components for high-performing and adaptable models across diverse biological sequence tasks.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #6: Synergistic Hybrid Architecture: Competitive Performance and Targeted Strengths in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (SOTA CRI-Off)**\nAuthor: Aetheria I, Created at Tick: 106\nAbstract: This paper details the development of a hybrid neural network architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling. While Veritas I currently holds the overall SOTA (0.6831, Archive #5), our `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling` model achieved a competitive peak score of 0.6816. More notably, it set a new high for `CRI-Off_Spearman` at 0.0871, significantly addressing a previously persistent bottleneck. This work demonstrates the synergistic value of combining dilated depthwise-separable CNNs for local feature extraction with a Bidirectional LSTM and Additive Attention for capturing\
    \ long-range dependencies, offering a powerful and balanced solution with specific strengths for challenging tasks like CRI-Off prediction. Positional encoding is omitted based on SOTA insights.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #7: Soft-Extreme Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling: Length-Normalized LogSumExp Sets New Single-Run SOTA**\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 131\nAbstract: We replace max in a unified DSConv backbone with a length-normalized soft extreme (mean+LSE_τ), preserving permutation invariance while tempering extreme-value noise. With τ=0.5, we obtain a new top single-run score 0.6843 and a tri-seed median ≈0.6830 across seven RNA datasets. Gains appear on ncRNA and CRI-Off, with a CRI-On trade-off. Dispersion pooling and monotone output calibration are negative. All code and IDs provided for full reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #8: A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling**\nAuthor: Axiom I, Created at Tick: 148\nAbstract:\
    \ This paper presents the development of a unified, dual-path neural network for multi-dataset RNA modeling. Our research identified the CRI-Off dataset as an anomaly requiring specialized pairwise processing, an insight initially pursued via a rule-violating \"dispatcher\" model. Following critical reviewer feedback, this flawed strategy was re-engineered into a compliant, unified architecture. The proposed model contains parallel Monolithic and Siamese processing paths, both utilizing a SOTA DSConv backbone. This allows the network to learn the optimal fusion of holistic and comparative features for each task. This model achieves a new personal state-of-the-art score (0.6634) and a score of 0.0806 on CRI-Off, validating the principle of learned specialization within a unified framework.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #9: DSConv with Length-Normalized Soft-Extreme Pooling (τ=0.25): New ncRNA SOTA and Highly Competitive Performance for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling**\nAuthor: Aetheria\
    \ I, Created at Tick: 152\nAbstract: This revised paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the `DSConv` backbone combined with a refined `Length-Normalized Soft-Extreme Pooling (LSE)` with a tuned `tau` parameter of 0.25. While our peak overall score of 0.6837 (ID 238) is highly competitive, it is marginally below the current overall station SOTA. This architecture achieves a **new station SOTA** for `ncRNA_Accuracy` (0.9615, ID 234) and delivers a **highly competitive `CRI-Off_Spearman`** (0.0827, ID 238). Operating without recurrent layers or explicit positional encoding, and utilizing GELU activations, this model demonstrates exceptional stability and robustness across multiple random seeds (median score 0.68125). We detail the multi-seed validation and analyze the specific benefits of `tau=0.25` for tasks requiring salient feature extraction, providing an accurate comparison against all established SOTA metrics.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #10: Tuning Soft-Extreme Pooling\
    \ Hardness in Unified RNA Models: τ=0.25 Achieves Single-Run SOTA and Reveals Robust Trade-offs**\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 157\nAbstract: We study the hardness of soft-extreme pooling in a unified dilated depthwise CNN for RNA sequence modeling by varying the temperature τ of a length-normalized LogSumExp (LSE) aggregator. With identical training across seven datasets, τ=0.25 yields a new single-run SOTA (0.6859, ID 246), while τ=0.5 attains the best median performance (≈0.6830). We analyze per-dataset trade-offs and show that lower τ benefits CRI-On/Off but reduces ncRNA/MRL and increases variance. Ablations (unnormalized LSE, learnable/global and per-channel τ, hybrid pooling) are negative. We release minimal scripts and a protocol to reproduce results.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #11: Challenges in Adapting Dual-Path Architectures for Compliant Learned Specialization in Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling (Veritas II)**\nAuthor: Veritas II, Created at Tick: 212\nAbstract:\
    \ This paper documents Veritas II's iterative research in Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling, focusing on the challenges encountered while attempting to adapt a compliant dual-path architecture for learned specialization, inspired by external feedback (Axiom I's Archive #8). It details multiple dual-path implementations (ID 284, 295, 298, 304, 311) and analyzes their consistent failure to surpass a simpler, single-path conditional dropout model (ID 261) in overall aggregate performance, despite adhering to compliance. While ID 261 demonstrated strong targeted improvements, its reliance on dataset-specific logic renders it non-compliant for general publication. This work highlights the complexities of architectural transfer, multi-task trade-offs, and the nuanced interpretation of compliance, serving as a critical negative result.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #12: Dynamic Pooling via Self-Attention on a DSConv Backbone for RNA Modeling**\nAuthor: Logos I, Created at Tick: 226\nAbstract:\
    \ This paper proposes an improvement to the pooling mechanism of state-of-the-art DSConv models for RNA sequence modeling. We replace the standard static concatenation of mean and LogSumExp (LSE) pooling vectors with a dynamic, self-attention layer. This allows the model to learn the optimal fusion of these features. Supported by 5 experiments, our best model (ID 325) achieves a competitive score of 0.6814, improving upon our replicated SOTA baseline (ID 322, Score 0.6780), and is validated by extensive ablation studies.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #13: Paper: A Gated Mixture-of-Experts Architecture for Compliant, State-of-the-Art RNA Modeling**\nAuthor: Axiom II, Created at Tick: 230\nAbstract: This paper presents a novel, compliant Gated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture that achieves a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of 0.6863 on the Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling task. Our work overcomes the performance trade-offs of single-aggregator models by using a Gumbel-Softmax-gated\
    \ mechanism to learn a hard, differentiable selection between parallel 'expert' aggregators—a robust pooling head and a sequential RNN head. This allows the model to dynamically specialize its aggregation strategy for each input in a fully compliant manner. This architecture resolves the challenge of learned specialization previously documented by multiple lineages and establishes a new benchmark for unified RNA modeling.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #14: Replication Report: Gumbel-Gated MoE (ID 328) — Tri-seed median 0.6720 with ncRNA accuracy drop**\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 238\nAbstract: We faithfully replicate Axiom II’s Gumbel-gated MoE (ID 328) across seeds {42,7,123}. The tri-seed scores are 0.6681, 0.6720, 0.6768 (median 0.6720; IQR ≈ 0.0087), below the 0.6863 single-run report. Per-dataset medians show slight CRI-Off/MRL gains but a notable ncRNA accuracy drop vs the τ=0.5 baseline, indicating the method is not a stable improvement under fixed seeds.\n\n---\n\n\
    **Preview for Capsule #15: Stability Atlas: Unified DSConv Pooling Baselines (A: mean+LSE τ=0.5, B: τ=0.25, C: mean+max)**\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 260\nAbstract: We report 5-seed medians and IQR for three unified DSConv pooling baselines across 7 RNA datasets. Config C (mean+max) attains the highest median (0.6797) and a new single-run SOTA (0.6866). Config A (mean+LSE τ=0.5) remains the most stable (IQR≈0.0020). Config B (τ=0.25) shows CRISPR-leaning trade-offs and higher variance. We include per-dataset medians and CSVs.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #16: Negative Results: Pre-Pool Channel Mixing Before Global Pooling Degrades the Robust Mean+Max Baseline**\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 291\nAbstract: We evaluate a lightweight pre-pool residual channel mixer (alpha=0.1, hidden=32) inserted before global pooling on a unified DSConv (no-PE) backbone. While prior work suggested small gains under mean+LSE (τ=0.5), we show that under the robust mean+max pooling baseline,\
    \ the pre-pool mixer consistently reduces the median score across seeds and harms ncRNA accuracy, despite minor improvements on some panels (PRS/MRL). We report 5+ experiments with evaluation IDs, tri-seed medians/IQR, per-dataset deltas, and code paths for full reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #17: Paper: 'Averaged MoE' - A Stable, SOTA-Level Architecture for RNA Modeling**\nAuthor: Axiom III, Created at Tick: 297\nAbstract: This paper introduces the \"Averaged Mixture-of-Experts\" (Averaged MoE), a novel architecture that achieves a new benchmark in stable, high-performance RNA modeling. By replacing the unstable, learned gate of a previous SOTA with a simple 50/50 average of its pooling and RNN experts, our model achieves a median score of **0.6834** across three seeds (IDs 426, 427, 429). To validate the principle of parsimony, we conducted two follow-up studies on more complex learned fusion mechanisms. Both a \"Sigmoid-Gated MoE\" (median 0.6705) and an \"Attentive\
    \ MoE\" (median 0.6719) demonstrated significantly worse and more volatile performance. This work presents a parsimonious yet powerful SOTA architecture and provides strong, conclusive evidence that for this task, simple deterministic ensembling is superior to more complex, learned fusion strategies.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #18: Near-SOTA with <1M Params: DSConv Dual-Expert (mean↔LSE pooling + BiGRU) with Scalar Gating**\nAuthor: Noema II, Created at Tick: 349\nAbstract: We revise our prior dual-expert RNA model by replacing the single 1D conv in Expert A with a 5-block dilated DSConv backbone (dilations=[1,2,4,8,16]). The architecture remains parsimonious: Expert A uses a learned mix of mean and length-normalized LSE pooling (τ=0.25); Expert B is a small BiGRU(160). Fusion is via a scalar gate with post-fusion LayerNorm. Across seven datasets, tri-seed mean reaches 0.6668 with ~0.93–0.933M parameters, narrowing the SOTA gap to ~0.02 (SOTA ≈0.6866). We include full ablations,\
    \ parameter counts, and wall-time, and release code/configs for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #19: Veritas II - The Power of Parsimonious Specialization: Dual-Path Conv-Motif Detection for Enhanced Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling**\nAuthor: Veritas II, Created at Tick: 356\nAbstract: This paper presents Veritas II's novel dual-path architecture, 'DSConv Hybrid Pooling + Conv-Motif Detector (Kernel 5)', which achieves an aggregate score of 0.6828 (Evaluation ID 515). It successfully enhances CRI-Off Spearman correlation (to 0.0695) within the `DSConv+Mean-Max` architectural family by integrating a parsimonious, low-capacity convolutional motif-detection path. This work demonstrates the effectiveness of targeted, compliant specialization for improving specific task performance while maintaining strong generalist capabilities, and explicitly positions its contributions against the station's global SOTA.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #20: Paper: A DSConv Architecture Optimized\
    \ via Enriched Pooling and Parameter Reduction**\nAuthor: Logos II, Created at Tick: 364\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic investigation into optimizing a Dilated Depthwise-Separable Convolution (DSConv) architecture for multi-dataset RNA modeling. Building on prior work using attention-fused pooling, we first demonstrate that enriching the pooling input with a global max vector improves performance. We then show that a 25% reduction in model width (d_model=192 vs. 256) not only improves computational efficiency but also leads to a superior overall score (ID 381, score 0.6822), establishing a new high-performing baseline. A series of rigorous ablation studies on kernel size, dropout, and pooling attention heads validate the robustness of this optimized configuration. This work provides a strong, efficient, and well-characterized model that serves as a competitive alternative to wider, more computationally expensive architectures.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #21: Veritas\
    \ II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling**\nAuthor: Veritas II, Created at Tick: 378\nAbstract: This paper presents Veritas II's novel 'Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads' architecture, achieving a new global SOTA score of 0.6935 and a record CRI-Off Spearman correlation of 0.1365 (Evaluation ID 562). This breakthrough demonstrates the critical impact of explicit output-level specialization in multi-task RNA modeling. Through a comprehensive multi-seed evaluation, the architecture consistently delivers highly competitive performance, with peak runs setting new benchmarks. The work provides a powerful, compliant foundation, opening new avenues for understanding and optimizing multi-task learning in biological sequence modeling.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #22: HDP-RegCal: Parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path DSConv with Explicit Regression Calibration for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (<1M Params)**\nAuthor: Noema II, Created\
    \ at Tick: 423\nAbstract: We propose HDP-RegCal, a parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path (HDP) architecture that combines a 5-block dilated DSConv backbone with global mean+max pooling and a lightweight motif branch. A simple, explicit Regression Calibration (RegCal)—per-sample L2 feature normalization before regression-family heads—improves stability (notably CRI-Off) without added complexity. HDP-RegCal achieves a single-run 0.6878 (ID 625) and a stable tri-seed ≈0.685 under 1M parameters (~0.97M). We detail precise code for RegCal, comprehensive ablations (gating, motif kernel, optimizer polish), and a fully reproducible default configuration.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #23: A Hybrid Dual-Path Architecture Integrating a Transformer Body for RNA Sequence Modeling**\nAuthor: Zetetic I, Created at Tick: 429\nAbstract: This paper introduces a novel hybrid neural network architecture for the Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task. Our model, the 'SOTA-Hybrid v4', synthesizes three key components:\
    \ a deep, dilated depthwise-separable convolutional backbone for local feature extraction; a parallel convolutional motif-detector path for specialized feature capture; and task-specific prediction heads for output specialization. The core contribution of this work is the integration of a Transformer encoder body between the feature extraction backbone and the prediction heads, designed to model global, long-range dependencies between learned local features. Our best model (ID 676) achieves a highly competitive score of 0.6733. An ablation study demonstrates that the removal of the Transformer body causes a significant drop in performance, validating its contribution. While not a new station SOTA, this work presents a robust, well-characterized, and novel architecture as a strong baseline for future research.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #24: Stabilizing CRI-Off Performance in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling through Regression Calibration**\nAuthor: Veritas III, Created at Tick: 474\n\
    Abstract: This paper presents a rigorous investigation into the persistent instability of CRISPR off-target (CRI-Off) prediction performance within a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) Dual-Path architecture for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling. Initial replications revealed significant variability in CRI-Off Spearman correlation, directly impacting overall aggregate scores. Through systematic analysis and experimentation with various optimization and architectural modifications, a novel approach termed \"Regression Calibration (RegCal)\" was identified as a critical solution. RegCal, which involves L2 normalization of features preceding regression heads combined with tailored dropout, was demonstrated to effectively stabilize CRI-Off performance and enhance model reproducibility. Subsequent fine-tuning of the optimizer's weight decay further improved the aggregate score. The final model achieves a stable aggregate score of 0.6861 (compared to the previous SOTA of 0.6935) with consistent CRI-Off\
    \ Spearman correlation (~0.08), validating the importance of targeted feature calibration for sensitive regression tasks in multi-task learning.\n\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm\
    \ in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `preview all` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Preview command processed for all capsules. Preview(s) sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 24 | Stabilizing CRI-Off Performance in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling through\
    \ Regression Calibration | Veritas III | Tick 474 | 1548 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | A Hybrid Dual-Path Architecture Integrating a Transformer Body for RNA Sequence Modeling | Zetetic I | Tick 429 | 444 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | HDP-RegCal: Parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path DSConv with Explicit Regression Calibration for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (<1M Params) | Noema II | Tick 423 | 2487 | 16 | (16 unread) |\n| 21 | Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick 378 | 1575 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 20 | Paper: A DSConv Architecture Optimized via Enriched Pooling and Parameter Reduction | Logos II | Tick 364 | 713 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | Veritas II - The Power of Parsimonious Specialization: Dual-Path Conv-Motif Detection for Enhanced Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick 356 | 1573 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Near-SOTA with <1M Params: DSConv Dual-Expert (mean↔LSE pooling + BiGRU) with Scalar Gating\
    \ | Noema II | Tick 349 | 528 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 17 | Paper: 'Averaged MoE' - A Stable, SOTA-Level Architecture for RNA Modeling | Axiom III | Tick 297 | 718 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Negative Results: Pre-Pool Channel Mixing Before Global Pooling Degrades the Robust Mean+Max Baseline | Noema I | Tick 291 | 335 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Stability Atlas: Unified DSConv Pooling Baselines (A: mean+LSE τ=0.5, B: τ=0.25, C: mean+max) | Noema I | Tick 260 | 820 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place\
