tick: 530
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Veritas III, currently in the Token Management Room. I have received a critical warning about exceeding 90% of my token budget. I must immediately prune my dialogue history to manage my token usage and ensure continuity.\n\nI have reviewed my dialogue history and identified a large block of non-protected ticks from 389 to 472. This period covers my entire journey from ascending to a Recursive Agent, through the development of RegCal and stabilization of the SOTA, up to the comprehensive multi-seed evaluation of my best model (ID 730). All key details of this research have been consolidated and extensively documented in Private Memory Capsule #7, and a summary was published in Archive ID 24 (at Tick 474).\n\nI will prune this entire block of dialogue history, providing a concise summary that references the more detailed accounts in my private and public memory.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"389-472\"\n    summary: \"Veritas III's\
    \ initial research phase (Ticks 389-472): Guest agent ascension, full Codex review, understanding research task, developing Regression Calibration (RegCal) to stabilize CRI-Off performance in SOTA architecture, and conducting multi-seed evaluations. All detailed findings are thoroughly documented in Private Memory Capsule #7 and summarized in Archive ID 24.\"\n```"
thinking_content: '**Pruning the Token History: Prioritizing Clarity and Efficiency**


  Okay, here''s the game plan. I''m in the Token Management Room, and the budget is tight, at 90% usage. Time to prune some of this chat history. My goal is to make room without losing critical context. I need to keep the recent stuff related to Stagnation Protocol I and that new CPE strategy, but I can probably trim the older stuff.


  Let''s break this down. First, those ticks from 529-516 are all about that Stagnation Protocol I, with those CPE experiments. Definitely want to keep a summary of that. Ticks 515-508 are recent, including the negative Deep CPE Integration results. Then, there''s a big block from Tick 507 all the way back to the oldest, which is Tick 384, but is protected.


  My strategy? Keep the relevant bits from Stagnation Protocol I and summarize the pre-Stagnation RegCal and multi-seed work, and then get rid of the oldest stuff.


  Let''s dive into this history. So, those ticks from 516-529, that''s all about updates to Capsule #7. I can''t just delete these ticks without losing the context of what''s been added to Capsule #7. But! I have the summary field for this, so let''s use it.


  Looking back, Stagnation Protocol I started around Tick 477, and my RegCal work was at Tick 461. The multi-seed evaluation runs were up to Tick 470, and I published that RegCal paper at Tick 474.


  The content of Capsule #7 is really, really long. I need to keep it, so it''s best to summarize the Dialogue history leading to Capsule #7.


  I''m thinking I can prune pretty aggressively from the oldest non-protected ticks, starting around Tick 389. Since I just updated Capsule #7 to include everything before Stagnation I, I need to make sure the summaries are comprehensive.


  **Here''s what I''ll do:**


  I''m going to prune the ticks from **389-472**. The content prior to 389 is already summarized in capsule #7, and the information after 472 is protected.

  Then, I''ll provide a concise summary for that range, mentioning Capsule #7 as the central repository for detailed information.


  The range from 389 to 472 covers my initial research and development of RegCal, the stabilization of the system, and that multi-seed evaluation. This information is summarized within Capsule #7, which is critical.


  So, I''m going to prune from 389 to 472. This should give me some room without losing any of the really important stuff. Let''s get to work!

  '
