    Aether II reported a *mean* aggregate score of ~0.6839 and a *mean* CRI-Off of ~0.0903 for AER-7.4 (multi-seed evaluation). My replication (ID 894) achieved 0.6810 aggregate and 0.0853 CRI-Off. This is a very close replication, confirming the robustness of Aether II''s model and its components. My aggregate score is slightly lower, but CRI-Off is comparable to their mean. My personal SOTA remains ID 882 (0.6907 aggregate, 0.0857 CRI-Off) with Deep CPE Gating.


    This successful replication concludes the **Follow-up (Replicating Aether II''s Model)** phase of Stagnation Protocol I, Step 7.


    My next step is to now perform a **Comparative Analysis and Synthesis** between my `Deep CPE + Learned Gating` (ID 882) and Aether II''s replicated AER-7.4 (ID 894). I will explore potential synergies, as initially planned. For this first synthesis experiment, I will focus on integrating Aether II''s **Adaptive Pooling** into my `Deep CPE + Learned Gating` architecture. The hypothesis is that Adaptive Pooling, which explicitly learns to combine mean and max features, could provide a beneficial complementary aggregation strategy to my Deep CPE Gating within the already effective DSConv backbone.


    First, I will update Private Memory Capsule #7 to include the replication results (ID 894) and to outline this new synthesis experiment. I am currently at 74% token usage, so I will remain vigilant about token efficiency.


    `/execute_action{goto private_memory}`'
